WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.480 --> 00:00:05.200
From banking ATMs to retail kiosk&nbsp;
services to manufacturing assembly lines,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:05.200 --> 00:00:08.480
intelligent devices are an integral&nbsp;
tool that we use to conduct business.&nbsp;

00:00:09.120 --> 00:00:13.680
To handle the scale of these deployments and&nbsp;
exploit their compute capacity, edge computing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:13.680 --> 00:00:17.920
brings enterprise applications closer to where&nbsp;
the data is created and actions need to be&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:17.920 --> 00:00:23.600
taken. It also allows enterprises to leverage&nbsp;
AI and analyze their data in near-real time.
&nbsp;

00:00:23.600 --> 00:00:28.320
While edge computing creates unique&nbsp;
opportunities, it also presents&nbsp;challenges.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:28.960 --> 00:00:32.480
By breaking down the neat physical&nbsp;
boundaries of cloud data centers,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:32.480 --> 00:00:37.120
edge computing requires companies to consider&nbsp;
security, management, and compliance for tens&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:37.120 --> 00:00:42.560
or even tens of thousands of offsite cameras,&nbsp;
sensors, and AI-ready factory controllers.

00:00:43.600 --> 00:00:48.000
The pervasiveness of these edge devices&nbsp;
can rapidly lead to a management problem&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:48.000 --> 00:00:49.920
that can't be handled without automation.

00:00:51.120 --> 00:00:54.720
Without autonomous management of these&nbsp;
edge devices, the diversity of your&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:54.720 --> 00:00:59.200
device inventory can also make traditional&nbsp;
software delivery costly and error prone.&nbsp;

00:00:59.920 --> 00:01:02.480
It's also important to remember&nbsp;
that these devices change often.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:03.120 --> 00:01:07.600
For example, they are reconfigured in the&nbsp;
field with more memory, storage, or connected&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:07.600 --> 00:01:12.560
to different sensors. They are re-purposed to&nbsp;
different tasks. They are moved or change owners.&nbsp;

00:01:13.360 --> 00:01:17.920
A final point to consider: without&nbsp;
autonomous management of the edge devices,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:17.920 --> 00:01:21.200
the lack of continuous connectivity&nbsp;
can lead to security exposures,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:21.200 --> 00:01:25.520
outages, or missed opportunities to provide&nbsp;
specialized offers to your customers. So,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:25.520 --> 00:01:30.240
your edge device management strategy must consider&nbsp;
devices that are available online or offline.

00:01:31.040 --> 00:01:34.720
Today I'll explain how IBM Edge&nbsp;
Application Manager can automate&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:34.720 --> 00:01:37.680
the deployment and maintenance&nbsp;
of your edge device inventory.&nbsp;

00:01:38.400 --> 00:01:42.880
You'll see how with autonomous management,&nbsp;
a single administrator can manage the scale,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:42.880 --> 00:01:45.120
variability, and rate of change of application&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:45.120 --> 00:01:47.520
environments across tens of&nbsp;
thousands of endpoints.&nbsp;&nbsp;
&nbsp;

00:01:48.640 --> 00:01:52.560
IBM Edge Application Manager allows you&nbsp;
to dramatically reduce the administrator’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:52.560 --> 00:01:57.440
burden by defining deployment intent instead of&nbsp;
coding and maintaining endless install scripts.

00:01:58.160 --> 00:02:03.120
It also manages your endpoints in one place&nbsp;
with the RedHat OpenShift platform that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:03.120 --> 00:02:07.440
runs on the cloud of your choice.
 
Okay - Imagine that you work for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:07.440 --> 00:02:12.160
a fictitious company, "World-of-Widgets".&nbsp;
You're about to roll out a new assembly line&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:12.160 --> 00:02:16.080
across your factories that can apply customized&nbsp;
painting for the widgets that you're producing.&nbsp;

00:02:17.040 --> 00:02:19.120
To ensure the quality of custom painting,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:19.120 --> 00:02:22.320
a set of programmable cameras are&nbsp;
installed on every assembly machine.

00:02:23.040 --> 00:02:25.840
However, this creates a&nbsp;
challenge for your IT department:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:25.840 --> 00:02:30.240
how do you manage all those devices and make sure&nbsp;
that they quickly adapt to new painting patterns?&nbsp;

00:02:31.120 --> 00:02:34.560
World-of-Widgets has decided to&nbsp;
use IBM Edge Application Manager.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:35.280 --> 00:02:38.400
IBM Edge Application Manager gives&nbsp;
an administrator the ability to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:38.400 --> 00:02:42.960
manage thousands of edge endpoints by&nbsp;
carrying out identification, agreement,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:42.960 --> 00:02:47.360
execution, and verification of&nbsp;
management actions without intervention,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:47.360 --> 00:02:52.400
driving down&nbsp;administration costs and streamlining&nbsp;
the deployment of device-specific updates.

00:02:53.120 --> 00:02:58.160
In this case, IBM Edge Application Manager helps&nbsp;
to deliver the service right to the cameras,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:58.160 --> 00:03:02.720
improving response time by spreading the&nbsp;workload&nbsp;
across hundreds of these small container-capable&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:02.720 --> 00:03:08.240
devices -- thereby avoiding the transport/compute&nbsp;
burden of a centralized data center architecture.

00:03:09.120 --> 00:03:12.320
It's the first week of the rollout&nbsp;
and the lead technician texts you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:12.320 --> 00:03:16.480
that they've&nbsp;just installed new machines&nbsp;
at the factory in Tucson, Arizona. Yippee!&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:16.480 --> 00:03:19.120
You're responsible for verifying that the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:19.120 --> 00:03:22.800
equipment has been successfully connected to the&nbsp;
management hub and that the right software gets&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:22.800 --> 00:03:24.720
installed on the edge devices.

00:03:24.720 --> 00:03:29.360
Fortunately, you don’t have to write scripts&nbsp;
with IBM Edge Application Manager. Instead,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:29.360 --> 00:03:34.080
you can specify the criteria&nbsp;that determines&nbsp;
what's deployed to each device -- you don't have&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:34.080 --> 00:03:36.800
to deal with each individual end point.

00:03:36.800 --> 00:03:41.600
The workload, defined as one or more services,&nbsp;
can be automatically deployed because it meets&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:41.600 --> 00:03:44.080
the criteria you've already set.

00:03:44.080 --> 00:03:48.640
Each service represents a set of specific&nbsp;
functionality and includes policies that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:48.640 --> 00:03:52.160
assure that the right software is placed&nbsp;
on the right node at the right time.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:52.160 --> 00:03:55.360
And the system will automatically update itself&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:55.360 --> 00:03:59.920
when new workloads or edge nodes are introduced,&nbsp;
or environmental conditions change that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:59.920 --> 00:04:02.960
invalidate the original placement decisions.

00:04:02.960 --> 00:04:08.000
As we've seen through this brief example, IBM&nbsp;
Edge Application Manager - with its autonomous&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:08.000 --> 00:04:11.360
management capability&nbsp;delivers&nbsp;
no touch lifecycle management.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:11.360 --> 00:04:14.000
When combined with an edge-native&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:14.000 --> 00:04:18.480
programming model, a single administrator&nbsp;
can securely deliver software to almost&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:18.480 --> 00:04:22.720
any smart device or cluster capable&nbsp;
of running containerized workloads.&nbsp;

